# Advertised rules that the defect-seed suite does not verify, and why.
#
# `packages/pi/src/standin/defect_seeds.zig` pairs a veto-clean baseline with a
# draft that introduces exactly one diagnostic code, and the stand-in gate
# re-derives each outcome through the real veto. A rule that no seed covers is
# a rule nothing in this repository has ever been observed enforcing.
#
# One row per (code, reason slug), with the finding above it. Enforced in both
# directions by the stand-in gate in packages/pi/src/standin_range_tests.zig,
# which runs under `zig build test-standin`: an advertised rule that is neither
# seeded nor listed fails, and so does a row for a code that a seed now
# verifies. The list only ratchets down.
#
# (This header named scripts/check-rule-coverage.sh, which does not exist. The
# gate has always been the Zig test above, so the reference pointed a reader at
# nothing while reading as though the enforcement were somewhere it could be
# inspected.)
#
# That gate keys on `rule.code`, which is what it must do to answer "is this
# advertised rule seeded". It therefore cannot see a registry row whose code
# has a live producer somewhere else - three dead enum variants sharing
# ZTS500/501/502 were permanently satisfied that way.
# scripts/check-diagnostic-producers.sh asks the other question.
#
# A row is not an exemption and not a plan. It is a claim, checked when it was
# written, that a seed cannot reach this code as the compiler stands today. The
# reason slug says what would have to change first, and every one of them is a
# defect somewhere other than the seed suite:
#
#   non-default     emitted only under an opt-in mode the veto does not run
#
# This file once carried nineteen rows in three categories. Seventeen are gone
# and none were closed by writing a seed:
#
#   - POL001/003/005/007 got a producer. The allow-list was enforced only at
#     build time, through a path raising `error.PolicyViolation` and printing
#     prose with no code, so `zts check` and the veto never saw it.
#   - ZTS306 got a producer. Unused-import was advertised and never wired.
#   - PROP01-06, ZTS300, ZTS301, ZTS307 and POL002/004/006/008 were deleted
#     from the registry. They were duplicate namings of coded findings, dead
#     enum variants, or diagnostics an earlier checker refuses first. A rule no
#     execution order can surface is a false advertisement in a hashed policy
#     surface, not defense in depth.
#
# Method, so a later reader can repeat it rather than trust it: each row was
# established by probe against the built analyzer, not by reading the registry.
# The handler the rule describes was written, `zts edit-simulate` was run
# against a clean baseline, and the codes it did report are named in the
# finding.

# ZTS508 is emitted only under the opt-in docs mode. The veto runs the default
# profile, so no seed measured through it can reach the rule. Reaching it needs
# a second analysis mode in the harness, not another draft.
ZTS508 non-default
